<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #d35400;">___</span> – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Are the Bananas?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences. Reading: Extract specific information from texts on familiar topics. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences at a farm stand (e.g., "I go to the market and see some fruit."). Can extract specific information from texts about farm products (e.g., "Papayas are on sale."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes, using knowledge of syllable segmentation (e.g., segmenting "banana" into "ba-na-na" with three syllables).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of various fruits and vegetables (e.g., banana, papaya, mango, lettuce, tomato, pineapple) introduced in Lesson 1. The teacher asks students to identify the items. "What is this? Is it a fruit or a vegetable?" "Where can we buy these?" (Expected answers: "It's a banana!", "Fruit!", "At the market/farm stand!"). The teacher then holds up a flashcard, points to it, and says, "How much is this banana?" prompting student engagement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite requests by holding up a flashcard of a papaya and saying, "Can you help me, please? I want to buy a papaya." The teacher then models asking about price, "How much is the papaya?" The teacher also models syllable segmentation for "banana" by clapping and saying "ba-na-na" (3 syllables) and "papaya" as "pa-pa-ya" (3 syllables), focusing on the /b/ and /p/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and phrases.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If I want to know the price of a mango, what do I say?" (Expected answer: "How much is the mango?")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If I need help at the market, what polite words do I use?" (Expected answer: "Can you help me, please?")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt titled "My Trip to the Farm Stand" (genre: simple story, register: informal, format: illustrated paragraph or short dialogue). The text describes a child's visit to a farm stand, seeing different fruits and vegetables, asking prices, and buying a few items. It incorporates vocabulary like <i>market, fruit, vegetable, banana, papaya, mango, price, buy, fresh, delicious, How much is...?, Can you help me, please?, some, any</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i><br>
                    "Hello! My name is Ana. Today I go to the local farm stand with my mom. It is a big market! I see many fresh fruits and vegetables. 'Look, Mom! Some yellow bananas!' I say. My mom asks the cashier, 'Can you help me, please? How much are the bananas?' The cashier says, 'The bananas are $1.00 for a bunch.' Then I see big green papayas. 'Do you have any ripe papayas?' I ask. The cashier answers, 'Yes, we have some delicious papayas. They are $2.00 each.' We buy some bananas and a big papaya. It is a healthy and cheap trip!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences. Extract specific information from texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Pre-reading/Gist & First Reading):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays the text and reads it aloud slowly, pointing to the words. Students follow along.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Where does Ana go with her mom?" (Students point to "farm stand" or say "farm stand / market")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to identify words with /p/ and /b/ sounds (e.g., "papaya", "banana", "buy").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity (first reading):</b> Students read the text again silently and answer two simple True/False questions.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Ana goes to the library with her mom. (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. They see fruits and vegetables. (True)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, identifying prices, recognizing polite requests and use of "some/any". Practice decoding English graphemes and phonemes through segmenting words into syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading Comprehension Questions):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the "My Trip to the Farm Stand" text and specific comprehension questions to answer by reading the text again.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does Ana see first? (Bananas)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How much are the bananas? ($1.00 for a bunch)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does Ana ask about the papayas? ("Do you have any ripe papayas?")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How much is one papaya? ($2.00)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What words in the text have 3 syllables? (banana, papaya)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Word Match):</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> Students complete sentences from the text using words from a word bank (e.g., <i>cashier, price, fresh, some, any</i>). Example: "We see many ____ fruits." "Do you have ____ ripe papayas?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Students read a list of words from the text (e.g., "banana", "papaya", "market", "tomato", "pineapple"). They clap for each syllable and write the number of syllables next to each word. The teacher monitors for correct segmenting and pronunciation of /p/ and /b/ sounds.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Read and Verify):</b> In pairs, students read their answers to the comprehension questions (from activity 1) to their partner. They check each other's answers by re-reading the relevant parts of the text together. The teacher circulates, offering help with reading and clarification.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Reading a Farm Stand Menu and Shopping List.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a "Farm Stand Menu" (a simple text listing items and their prices, e.g., "Bananas: $1.00 a bunch", "Papayas: $2.00 each", "Lettuce: $0.75", "Eggs: $3.00 a dozen", "Milk: $1.50") and a "Customer's Shopping List" (a short text with items a customer wants to buy, e.g., "Need 1 bunch of bananas, 2 papayas, some eggs.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads their "Farm Stand Menu" to understand the prices and availability of items.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they read the "Customer's Shopping List."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to read both texts and calculate the total cost for the shopping list items. They must write down the items and their individual prices from the menu, then the total. This requires extracting specific numerical information and item names from the texts.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes, checking for students' ability to read and extract information accurately, use of target vocabulary, and comprehension of "How much is...?" implicitly.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Short Reading Quiz.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually complete a short "Farm Stand Order" reading quiz. They read 3-4 short sentences or questions related to the "My Trip to the Farm Stand" narrative or a new very short menu, and write their answers.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Maria wants to buy a healthy fruit. The bananas are $1.00. The mangoes are $1.50. Which fruit is cheaper?" (Students write: Bananas)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "How many syllables are in the word 'pineapple'?" (Students write: 3)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to extract specific information from a short text (e.g., prices, item names).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly answering questions about the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates phonemic awareness by correctly segmenting words.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following sentences:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to read about..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading about prices at the farm stand was easy / a little difficult because..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read today? Can you spell one?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting thing you read about the farm stand?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., students were engaged with the story, they could extract prices, pronunciation practice was effective). What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more visual support for new vocabulary, explicit strategy for identifying main ideas, additional practice with "some/any" in reading texts). Did students grasp the pronunciation of /p/ and /b/ and syllable segmentation for words like "banana" and "papaya" while reading?</p>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about the farm stand. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this knowledge by having students actively speak and interact using the target vocabulary and grammar to ask and answer questions about prices and availability at a farm stand scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>